(function () {
  window.AIPO = window.AIPO || {};
  window.AIPO.lessons = window.AIPO.lessons || [];
  window.AIPO.scenarios = window.AIPO.scenarios || {};
  window.AIPO.why = window.AIPO.why || {};
  window.AIPO.questions = window.AIPO.questions || [];
  window.AIPO.cards = window.AIPO.cards || [];
  function S(id, d, stem, options, correct, explanation, scenario) { var q = { id: id, domain: d, type: "single", stem: stem, options: options, correct: [correct], explanation: explanation, reviewed: false }; if (scenario) q.scenario = scenario; return q; }
  function M(id, d, pick, stem, options, correct, explanation, scenario) { var q = { id: id, domain: d, type: "multi", pick: pick, stem: stem, options: options, correct: correct, explanation: explanation, reviewed: false }; if (scenario) q.scenario = scenario; return q; }

  window.AIPO.lessons.push(
    {
      "id": "frame-1",
      "n": 1,
      "domain": "frame",
      "title": "Start from the user problem, not the technology",
      "body": "Many AI efforts begin with a sentence such as 'we need an AI feature'. That starts from a solution. The Google People + AI Guidebook, in its chapter User Needs + Defining Success (paraphrased here as concept guidance), suggests another opening: find a real problem that people need help with, then ask how it might be solved and whether AI could solve it in a unique way. Compare 'Can we use AI to...?' with 'How might we solve...?'. The second keeps every option open, including non-AI ones.\n\nTalking to people, observing behaviour and looking through data are the usual ways to find it. Mapping the current workflow step by step shows where effort, delay or error really sit and which steps could be automated or helped. PAIR names a 'Wizard of Oz' test as a way to see how people feel about automating part of a process; in common usage a person performs the step behind the scenes, which is general practice and lets a team learn before building. Hear from a diverse set of users early, so that some groups are not left out by accident.\n\nNIST AI RMF 1.0 supports the same habit. MAP 1.1 asks that intended purposes, the types of users and their expectations, and potential positive and negative impacts are understood and documented. MAP 1.4 asks that the business value is clearly defined, or re-evaluated when an existing system is assessed. As practical judgement, when a problem statement rests on opinion alone, the PO should pause and gather evidence before committing to a solution.",
      "points": [
        "Ask 'How might we solve this problem?' before 'Can we use AI?'.",
        "Find the problem through conversations, observation and data.",
        "Map the current workflow to see where help is really needed.",
        "Test reactions to automation cheaply, for example with a simulated step.",
        "Document purpose, users, expectations and business value (MAP 1.1, 1.4)."
      ],
      "traps": [
        "Starting from a sponsor's wish for 'an AI feature'.",
        "Treating a stakeholder's opinion as a confirmed user problem.",
        "Interviewing only the easiest or most vocal users.",
        "Never revisiting the business value of a feature already live."
      ],
      "sources": [
        "Google People + AI Guidebook, chapter User Needs + Defining Success (concepts paraphrased guidance; the local PDF may be an older edition than the live guidebook)",
        "NIST AI RMF 1.0 (AI 100-1, Jan 2023), MAP 1.1",
        "NIST AI RMF 1.0 (AI 100-1, Jan 2023), MAP 1.4",
        "General practice, not from a source"
      ],
      "sourceVersion": "AI RMF 1.0, Jan 2023; PAIR guidebook (edition may differ from live)",
      "reviewed": false
    },
    {
      "id": "frame-2",
      "n": 2,
      "domain": "frame",
      "title": "Does AI add unique value, or would a rule do?",
      "body": "Once a real problem is clear, the next question is whether AI is the right tool. A rule or heuristic is a fixed piece of logic written by people, such as 'if the field is empty, show an error'. The PAIR chapter User Needs + Defining Success (paraphrased) points out that a rule often works as well as an AI version, or better, and is easier to build, explain, debug and maintain. A simpler non-AI approach is therefore a legitimate and frequently correct answer.\n\nThe chapter lists situations where AI is probably the better fit: personalised recommendations, predicting future events, understanding natural language, recognising a whole class of things, and detecting rare events that keep changing, such as fraud. It also lists situations where AI is probably not better: when predictability is the value, when information is static or limited, when costly errors outweigh a small gain, when complete transparency is needed, when speed and low cost matter above all, and when people have said they do not want the task changed.\n\nNIST AI RMF 1.0 points the same way. MAP 2.1 asks that the specific tasks, and the methods used to implement them, are defined, for example classifiers, generative models or recommenders. MANAGE 2.1 asks that managing risk takes account of viable non-AI alternatives that could reduce the size or likelihood of impacts. As good practice, test a simple baseline first, and let AI earn its place by showing it adds value beyond it.",
      "points": [
        "A rule or heuristic is often simpler to build, explain, debug and maintain.",
        "AI tends to fit personalisation, prediction, language, class recognition and changing rare events.",
        "AI is probably not better where predictability, transparency or very costly errors dominate.",
        "Define the specific tasks and methods the system will support (MAP 2.1).",
        "Consider viable non-AI alternatives when managing risk (MANAGE 2.1)."
      ],
      "traps": [
        "Choosing AI because it is modern or because competitors have it.",
        "Comparing AI options only against each other, never against a simple rule.",
        "Accepting a small average gain while ignoring severe rare errors.",
        "Treating 'no AI' as a failure of ambition."
      ],
      "sources": [
        "Google People + AI Guidebook, chapter User Needs + Defining Success (concepts paraphrased guidance; the local PDF may be an older edition than the live guidebook)",
        "NIST AI RMF 1.0 (AI 100-1, Jan 2023), MAP 2.1",
        "NIST AI RMF 1.0 (AI 100-1, Jan 2023), MANAGE 2.1",
        "General practice, not from a source"
      ],
      "sourceVersion": "AI RMF 1.0, Jan 2023; PAIR guidebook (edition may differ from live)",
      "reviewed": false
    },
    {
      "id": "frame-3",
      "n": 3,
      "domain": "frame",
      "title": "Automate the task or augment the person?",
      "body": "When AI is the right approach, there is still a design choice. Automation means the system does the task for the person. Augmentation means the system helps the person do the task themselves, faster or better. The PAIR chapter User Needs + Defining Success (paraphrased) suggests automation when a task is boring, repetitive, awkward or dangerous, when people lack the knowledge or ability to do it, or when they simply want it gone. It is helped further when the people who do the task today agree on the correct way to do it.\n\nIt suggests augmentation when people enjoy the task, when personal responsibility for the outcome matters, when the stakes are high for the person, or when their preferences are hard to put into words. If people say plainly that they do not want a task automated or helped by AI, treat that as a signal worth exploring before committing.\n\nEven when a task is automated, the chapter says there should almost always be a way for people to oversee it and step in, for example by previewing, editing or undoing. The research questions it suggests, such as which duties a person would give to a human assistant, help to learn what people want to hand over. Where a personal responsibility may be required by law, that is a question for legal and compliance specialists, not one the PO settles alone.",
      "points": [
        "Automate tasks that are tedious, dangerous, beyond people's skill or agreed in method.",
        "Augment tasks people enjoy, own personally, find high stakes or struggle to describe.",
        "Ask users which duties they would give a human assistant.",
        "Keep a route for oversight and intervention even in automated tasks.",
        "Involve legal and compliance specialists when responsibility may be a legal matter."
      ],
      "traps": [
        "Assuming automation is always the best help for users.",
        "Ignoring people who say they do not want the task changed.",
        "Automating with no way to preview, edit or undo.",
        "Settling a possible legal responsibility question without specialists."
      ],
      "sources": [
        "Google People + AI Guidebook, chapter User Needs + Defining Success (concepts paraphrased guidance; the local PDF may be an older edition than the live guidebook)",
        "General practice, not from a source"
      ],
      "sourceVersion": "AI RMF 1.0, Jan 2023; PAIR guidebook (edition may differ from live)",
      "reviewed": false
    },
    {
      "id": "frame-4",
      "n": 4,
      "domain": "frame",
      "title": "Define success with the team: the reward function and the cost of errors",
      "body": "Every AI model is guided by something that tells it what counts as right or wrong. In PAIR terms (chapter User Needs + Defining Success, paraphrased) this is the reward function, also called the objective or loss function. It shapes what the model tries to optimise and so much of the user experience. PAIR says it should be designed together by a cross-functional team, with UX, product and engineering views at a minimum, and imagines downstream effects over the long term.\n\nA key step is weighing errors. A false positive is a wrong 'yes', such as a false alarm. A false negative is a wrong 'no', such as a missed case. Treating both as equal by default rarely matches real consequences, so the team should discuss which hurts more. Precision and recall trade against each other, and PAIR notes there is no way around that trade-off, so test the balance with users.\n\nA simple, narrow, immediate measure can cause harm over time. A useful check is to ask what would happen to users and to society if the measure were perfectly optimised. PAIR suggests agreeing a statement of the form: if a specific metric crosses a meaningful threshold, we will take a specific action. It also suggests checking impact on a regular schedule, and speaking with any group that is harmed. The PO helps the team agree this definition. Model evaluation itself is covered in domain 5.",
      "points": [
        "The reward function defines what the model optimises and shapes the user experience.",
        "Design it with UX, product and engineering together, thinking long term.",
        "Weigh false positives and false negatives by their real consequences.",
        "Precision and recall trade off, so test the balance with users.",
        "Agree a metric, a threshold and an action, and check impact on a schedule."
      ],
      "traps": [
        "Using one simple, immediate measure such as time spent or shares.",
        "Weighing both kinds of error equally by default.",
        "Letting data scientists define success alone.",
        "Setting no action to take when the measure goes wrong."
      ],
      "sources": [
        "Google People + AI Guidebook, chapter User Needs + Defining Success (concepts paraphrased guidance; the local PDF may be an older edition than the live guidebook)",
        "General practice, not from a source"
      ],
      "sourceVersion": "AI RMF 1.0, Jan 2023; PAIR guidebook (edition may differ from live)",
      "reviewed": false
    },
    {
      "id": "frame-5",
      "n": 5,
      "domain": "frame",
      "title": "Write it down: purpose, benefits, costs of errors and scope",
      "body": "Framing is not finished until it is written down where others can see it. NIST AI RMF 1.0 is useful here. MAP 1.1 asks that intended purposes, beneficial uses, types of users and their expectations, potential positive and negative impacts, and assumptions and limitations are understood and documented. MAP 3.1 asks that the potential benefits of the intended functionality and performance are examined and documented. MAP 3.2 asks the same for potential costs of expected or realised errors, including non-monetary costs, connected to organisational risk tolerance.\n\nNon-monetary costs are those without a price tag, such as lost trust, distress, unfair treatment or harm to a person. A case listing only benefits cannot show that the trade-off is worthwhile. MAP 1.5 asks that organisational risk tolerances are determined and documented, and GOVERN 2.3 says executive leadership takes responsibility for decisions about AI risks. As practical judgement, the PO therefore does not set risk tolerance alone, and brings evidence and escalates.\n\nFinally, MAP 3.3 asks that the targeted application scope is specified and documented, based on the system's capability, established context and categorisation. As practical judgement, a clear scope also says what the system will not do. As practical judgement, a new language, group or task is a new condition: treat it as out of scope until it has been evaluated, and involve the relevant specialists when it is high stakes or touches personal data.",
      "points": [
        "Document purpose, users, expectations, impacts and assumptions (MAP 1.1).",
        "Examine and document benefits (MAP 3.1) and costs of errors (MAP 3.2).",
        "Include non-monetary costs such as trust, distress and harm.",
        "Settle risk tolerance with the people accountable for risk.",
        "State scope from capability and context, including what is out of scope (MAP 3.3)."
      ],
      "traps": [
        "A business case with benefits and no costs.",
        "Counting only monetary costs of errors.",
        "Setting risk tolerance alone as the PO.",
        "Letting scope grow to new users or tasks without evaluation."
      ],
      "sources": [
        "NIST AI RMF 1.0 (AI 100-1, Jan 2023), MAP 1.1",
        "NIST AI RMF 1.0 (AI 100-1, Jan 2023), MAP 1.5",
        "NIST AI RMF 1.0 (AI 100-1, Jan 2023), MAP 3.1",
        "NIST AI RMF 1.0 (AI 100-1, Jan 2023), MAP 3.2",
        "NIST AI RMF 1.0 (AI 100-1, Jan 2023), MAP 3.3",
        "NIST AI RMF 1.0 (AI 100-1, Jan 2023), GOVERN 2.3",
        "General practice, not from a source"
      ],
      "sourceVersion": "AI RMF 1.0, Jan 2023",
      "reviewed": false
    }
  );

  window.AIPO.scenarios.scFR1 = {"title":"Reducing missed clinic appointments","text":"A clinic network loses many appointment slots to patients who do not attend. The scheduling lead proposes an AI system that predicts likely no-shows and automatically double-books those slots. So far the team has no notes from conversations with patients or front-desk staff, and the only data is last year's booking export, which includes some health-related details. Front-desk staff say most misses happen because patients forget, and reminders currently go out only by letter. The privacy team has not yet been consulted."};
  window.AIPO.scenarios.scFR2 = {"title":"An internal help assistant for expense questions","text":"An organisation plans an internal help assistant for expense questions. Support tickets show that about half of all questions repeat the same dozen policy questions, each with a fixed answer. The rest are unusual cases that need a judgement by the finance team. The sponsor's stated goal is fewer tickets. The policy wording changes a few times a year. A wrong answer could lead an employee to submit a claim that is rejected or that breaks a rule."};

  window.AIPO.questions.push(
    S("fr001", "frame", "A sponsor tells a Product Owner, 'We need an AI feature in the app this quarter.' Which first step is best?", ["Ask engineering which AI technique would give the quickest demo","Draft a backlog of model features and size the stories so the first sprint can begin","Ask which user problem the feature should solve and how people cope with it today","Pick a supplier so that work can start without delay"], 2, "Starting from the user problem keeps every solution open, including ones without AI. A technique, a backlog or a supplier all assume the answer before the problem is understood."),
    S("fr002", "frame", "Which question matches the PAIR starting point for exploring human-centred AI solutions?", ["How might we solve this, and can AI solve it uniquely?","Can we use AI to speed this process up for our internal team?","Which AI approach is most popular for ideas like ours right now?","What would it cost us to copy a rival's AI feature this year?"], 0, "PAIR contrasts 'Can we use AI to...?' with 'How might we solve...?' and then asks whether AI can solve it in a unique way. The first form begins with the technology."),
    S("fr003", "frame", "A stakeholder insists users hate the checkout page, but no one has watched users or reviewed data. What should the PO do?", ["Commission an AI model to personalise the page","Redesign the page to match the stakeholder's view","Add the issue to the backlog as a confirmed problem","Gather evidence first, by observing users and reviewing behaviour data, before choosing a fix"], 3, "A problem statement needs evidence. Observing people and looking at data can shift thinking from technology-first to people-first, and may show the real cause is something else."),
    S("fr004", "frame", "What is the main benefit of mapping how people complete a task today, step by step?", ["It proves that AI is the right answer for the task","It shows where the effort, delay or error really sits and which steps could be automated or helped","It gives the model team a ready-made training set","It replaces the need to talk to the people involved, since the map already records everything they do"], 1, "A workflow map reveals steps and pain points so the team can see where help is needed. It does not decide the solution, supply data on its own or replace talking to users."),
    M("fr005", "frame", 2, "Which TWO activities best move a team from technology-first to people-first thinking?", ["Observe people doing the task in their own setting","Benchmark which AI features competitors have released","Ask engineers which techniques they would like to try","Review data on how the task is done today","Write the model requirements before meeting any users"], [0,3], "Talking to people, observing behaviour and looking through data ground the problem in real needs. Competitor features, engineer preferences and early model requirements start from the solution side."),
    S("fr006", "frame", "A team wants to learn whether people would accept an automated step before building it. Which approach fits?", ["Build a full model first, then ask users for feedback","Survey stakeholders for their best guess about whether users would accept the automated step","Have a person carry out the step behind the scenes and watch how users react","Skip testing, since users will adapt after launch"], 2, "PAIR names the Wizard of Oz test as a way to see how people feel about automating part of a process. The usual meaning, a person quietly performing the step, is general practice rather than PAIR wording, and it lets a team learn before committing to build."),
    S("fr007", "frame", "A team proposes an AI model to check that a postcode field has the right format. What is the best response?", ["Use a simple rule, because fixed formats do not need AI","Approve it, since AI will catch more edge cases over time","Approve it, to build the team's AI experience","Run a model comparison and let the best score decide"], 0, "When information is static and limited, a rule is easier to build, explain, debug and maintain. PAIR lists this as a case where AI is probably not better."),
    S("fr008", "frame", "Which situation is the strongest case for using AI instead of fixed rules?", ["Showing the same fixed opening hours to every visitor of the site","Keeping a Cancel button in the same place on each screen of the app","Applying a published fixed fee table to an order at checkout time","Spotting new fraud that is rare per person and keeps changing"], 3, "PAIR lists detection of low-occurrence events that change over time as a good fit. Static information, predictable controls and fixed tables suit rules."),
    S("fr009", "frame", "An AI route suggestion would save drivers a few seconds but sometimes send them down unsafe tracks. What does the framing guidance point to?", ["Launch with AI, since its average accuracy is higher than the simpler approach","Keep the simpler approach, since very costly errors can outweigh a small gain","Launch with AI and fix unsafe cases as they appear","Ask the model team to raise the success rate slightly"], 1, "PAIR lists minimising costly errors as a situation where AI is probably not better: a small increase in success rate does not justify a very high cost of errors."),
    M("fr010", "frame", 2, "Which TWO situations suggest AI is probably NOT the better choice?", ["Different users need different personalised suggestions","Users or developers must understand precisely what the system does","The system must understand natural language in many speech styles","A whole class of items must be recognised, not just known examples","The most valuable part of the experience is that it behaves predictably"], [1,4], "PAIR lists complete transparency and predictability among situations where a rule is probably better. Personalisation, language understanding and class recognition are cases where AI often adds value."),
    S("fr011", "frame", "Under MANAGE 2.1 in the NIST AI RMF, what should be weighed alongside the resources needed to manage AI risks?", ["Whether a competitor has launched a similar feature","How much effort the team has already spent","Viable non-AI alternative approaches that could reduce the size or likelihood of harm","Which model is rated highest on public leaderboards"], 2, "MANAGE 2.1 asks that managing risk take into account resources and viable non-AI alternative systems, approaches or methods to reduce the magnitude or likelihood of impacts."),
    S("fr012", "frame", "The business needs something in users' hands within weeks. A rule-based version can ship in time and an AI version cannot. What is the best decision?", ["Ship the rule-based version first, then revisit AI if needed","Wait until the AI version is ready, however long that delay takes","Ship a rushed AI version now and test it live with real users","Cancel the work until an AI version becomes feasible to build"], 0, "When speed to market matters most, a simpler version can deliver value now. PAIR lists speed and low cost as a case where AI may not be the better choice. Evidence can justify AI later."),
    S("fr013", "frame", "A team says AI is needed because it is modern. Which request from the PO is most useful?", ["Ask the team to document the AI vision for stakeholders","Ask for a list of the AI features that other products have released this year","Ask engineering to start training a model in parallel","Ask how AI adds value that a tested rule-based baseline would not"], 3, "This is general practice, in line with PAIR: question whether AI will improve the experience and compare against a simple baseline before committing."),
    S("fr014", "frame", "Staff must copy figures between two systems all day. They find it boring and all agree on the correct way to do it. What does PAIR suggest?", ["Augment it, so staff can take pride in every figure they copy across","Automate it, with preview, edit or undo options for oversight","Leave it manual, since staff know the rules","Automate it fully with no way to intervene"], 1, "Tedious, repetitive tasks with an agreed correct method are good automation candidates. PAIR adds that even automated tasks should almost always allow human oversight and intervention."),
    S("fr015", "frame", "A team wants AI to write every note a counsellor sends to clients. Counsellors say the personal tone is the heart of their work. What is the best direction?", ["Automate the notes and monitor the client complaints that follow","Automate only the notes that are sent outside normal office hours","Augment: draft help, with the counsellor staying in control","Automate the notes first, then ask counsellors to adapt to them"], 2, "People prefer control when a task carries personal responsibility, high stakes or personal value. Augmentation supports the person without removing their role."),
    S("fr016", "frame", "A team plans to automate a task. Which design choice does PAIR say should almost always be offered?", ["A way for people to oversee and step in, such as preview or undo","A promise that the AI will not make mistakes","A hidden switch known only to engineers","A monthly summary report sent to the sponsor and the steering group"], 0, "PAIR says automation should almost always come with an option for human oversight and intervention, for example previewing, testing, editing or undoing."),
    M("fr017", "frame", 2, "Which TWO signs point towards augmenting the person rather than automating the task?", ["The task is boring and repetitive","The task is unsafe for a person to do","People enjoy the task and want to take part in it","The task is high stakes for the person doing it","People lack the knowledge or skill to do it"], [2,3], "PAIR suggests augmenting when people enjoy the task, carry personal responsibility, face high stakes or find preferences hard to put into words. Boring, dangerous or beyond-skill tasks favour automation."),
    S("fr018", "frame", "Interviewed staff say they do not want a particular task automated or helped by AI, but the sponsor wants to proceed. What is the best response?", ["Proceed and rely on staff to adapt to the change after launch","Proceed but keep the AI hidden from the staff who are directly involved","Drop the sponsor's goal entirely and ask staff for new ideas","Explore why, then look for a route that leaves that task alone"], 3, "PAIR notes that if people explicitly say they do not want a task automated or augmented with AI, that is a good task not to try to disrupt. Exploring their reasons and looking for another way to meet the sponsor's goal respects that signal."),
    S("fr019", "frame", "A team wants to fully automate a task in which the law may require a named person to take responsibility. What is the best next step?", ["Automate it now and then record a named person against each task afterwards","Involve legal and compliance specialists before deciding on automation","Ask the data science team to confirm the rule","Defer to the sponsor's reading of the requirement"], 1, "PAIR notes personal responsibility is sometimes legally required. This is not for the PO to settle alone. The right move is to involve the people with legal expertise."),
    S("fr020", "frame", "In PAIR terms, what does the reward function of an AI model define?", ["How the product is priced and packaged for users","How many users the feature is expected to reach","How the model tells right from wrong outputs, which shapes what it tries to optimise","How the team will be rewarded if the launch succeeds"], 2, "The reward function, also called objective or loss function, is how a model determines right and wrong predictions and so what behaviour it optimises. It is a major driver of the user experience."),
    S("fr021", "frame", "Who should help design the reward function, according to PAIR?", ["A cross-functional group that includes at least UX, product and engineering","The data scientists, who know the maths best","The sponsor alone, who owns the budget","The vendor supplying the model, since its default settings have been widely tested"], 0, "PAIR says defining success is a collaborative process across disciplines, with UX, product and engineering perspectives at a minimum, to reveal pitfalls where the function optimises the wrong outcome."),
    S("fr022", "frame", "A tool will flag urgent safety messages for staff. Which approach to errors is best?", ["Weigh false alarms and misses equally by default","Minimise false alarms to keep staff comfortable","Let the model's training process pick the balance","Discuss with staff which error is worse, then weigh a missed urgent message more heavily"], 3, "PAIR warns that weighing errors equally by default rarely matches real consequences. The cost of a miss and of a false alarm should be discussed and weighted deliberately."),
    S("fr023", "frame", "A team's only success measure is minutes spent in the app. What is the best challenge from the PO?", ["Accept it, since more time in the app means more value is delivered","Ask what happens to users over time if this is perfectly optimised","Add a second activity count to confirm it","Replace it with a count of new sign-ups"], 1, "PAIR warns that simple, narrow, immediate measures can have bad long-term effects, and suggests asking what happens to users and society if the function were perfectly optimised."),
    M("fr024", "frame", 2, "Which TWO belong in a team's agreed statement of success and failure for an AI feature?", ["A specific metric with a meaningful threshold","The name of the model that will be used","The action the team will take if the threshold is crossed","The launch date announced to stakeholders","A list of the vendors considered"], [0,2], "PAIR suggests a simple statement: if a specific metric for the feature crosses a meaningful threshold, we will take a specific action. That keeps everyone aligned on success, failure and who acts."),
    S("fr025", "frame", "After launch, a team finds the feature works less well for one user group. What best fits PAIR's guidance on monitoring negative impact?", ["Note it as an edge case and move on to the next release item","Wait for the overall average score to drop before taking any action","Talk with that group, find the cause, and adapt the product","Remove that group's data from the reports so metrics look stable"], 2, "PAIR advises checking impact metrics on a schedule and, when some stakeholders are harmed, talking to them and adapting the product. It also asks teams to assess inclusivity."),
    S("fr026", "frame", "Which set of items best matches what MAP 1.1 asks teams to understand and document about a system's context?", ["Intended purposes, the users and their expectations, and possible positive and negative impacts","Model type, supplier, hosting region and release cadence","Budget, team members, sprint dates and risks logged","Marketing message, price band and target revenue"], 0, "MAP 1.1 covers intended purposes, beneficial uses, the types of users and expectations, potential positive and negative impacts, and assumptions and limitations. The other lists are about delivery."),
    M("fr027", "frame", 2, "Which TWO should be written down before building an AI feature, in line with MAP 3.1 and 3.2?", ["The final choice of model settings","The potential benefits of the intended functionality","The names of everyone who attended a workshop","The potential costs of expected or actual errors","The tool used to track tasks"], [1,3], "MAP 3.1 asks for benefits to be examined and documented, and MAP 3.2 asks the same for costs of errors, including non-monetary costs. Settings, attendance and tools are not part of that framing."),
    S("fr028", "frame", "An AI feature was launched two years ago and nobody can say what business value it delivers. Which action fits MAP 1.4?", ["Leave it alone, because it has run without complaints","Expand it to more users and teams, hoping that the value will then show itself clearly","Add more training data to improve it","Re-evaluate the business value and context of use, then decide whether to continue"], 3, "MAP 1.4 asks that business value be clearly defined or, for an existing system, re-evaluated. Without a known value, continuing or expanding is a guess."),
    S("fr029", "frame", "A PO believes errors from a planned feature could seriously harm users, but the organisation has not said how much risk it will accept. What is the best action?", ["Set a tolerance on your own and record it in the product backlog today","Escalate to the people accountable for risk and agree a tolerance","Proceed with the build and review the risk once the first release is out","Ask the engineers to decide on an acceptable error rate for the product"], 1, "MAP 3.2 links costs of errors to organisational risk tolerance, MAP 1.5 asks that tolerances be determined and documented, and GOVERN 2.3 says executive leadership takes responsibility for risk decisions. As practical judgement, the PO supplies evidence and escalates."),
    S("fr030", "frame", "A draft business case lists benefits in detail but says nothing about what happens when the system is wrong. What is the best response?", ["Approve it, since errors can be handled in testing","Approve it, since benefits clearly outweigh costs","Add the costs of errors, including non-monetary costs, before approving the work","Ask the team to rewrite the benefits section so that it sounds even more convincing"], 2, "MAP 3.1 and 3.2 ask for both benefits and costs to be examined. A case with only benefits cannot show that the trade-off is worthwhile."),
    S("fr031", "frame", "According to MAP 3.3, what should the targeted application scope of an AI system be based on?", ["The system's capability, its established context and how it is categorised","The most ambitious ask that stakeholders have made about what the system should do","The size of the budget remaining","The scope used by the nearest competitor"], 0, "MAP 3.3 asks that scope be specified and documented based on capability, established context and AI system categorisation."),
    S("fr032", "frame", "A team wants to offer an assistant in a language that was never tested. What is the best decision?", ["Include it, since the model is the same","Include it, with a small notice telling users that results may vary a lot","Ask users in that language to report problems","Keep that language out of scope until it has been evaluated"], 3, "MAP 3.3 asks that scope be specified and documented from capability and established context. As practical judgement, results in tested conditions do not carry over automatically, so an untested language stays out of scope until it has been evaluated."),
    S("fr033", "frame", "Why is it useful to state what an assistant will NOT do, as well as what it will do?", ["It lets the team skip all testing for requests outside the stated scope","It sets clear limits so users and teams know when to look elsewhere","It removes the need for any human fallback","It helps the product look more capable"], 1, "A clear out-of-scope statement helps users and teams know where the system can be relied on. It does not remove the need for testing within scope or for fallback routes."),
    S("fr034", "frame", "A stakeholder asks to extend a working internal assistant to answer health questions from staff. What is the best response?", ["Switch it on, since the assistant is already live","Add a disclaimer and extend the scope","Treat it as a new use: frame it, involve specialists, keep it out of scope","Let users try it and watch for complaints"], 2, "A new use has new users, impacts and costs of error. It needs its own framing and the right expertise, so it stays out of the current scope until that is done."),
    S("fr035", "frame", "What should the PO do first?", ["Talk to front-desk staff and patients and analyse why visits are missed before picking a solution","Start training a prediction model on last year's booking export and then plan the double-booking rules","Ask the vendor shortlist for demos of no-show prediction","Write requirements for automatic double-booking"], 0, "The sponsor has proposed a solution without evidence for the cause. Staff say misses come from forgetting, so gathering evidence about causes comes before any solution.", "scFR1"),
    S("fr036", "frame", "Staff believe most misses are simple forgetting. Which option should be tested first?", ["A prediction model that is retuned every week as new bookings come in","A chatbot that lets patients rebook their own slots whenever they ask","A redesigned booking page with an AI layer for personalised slot offers","Timely text or phone reminders, tested against the current letters"], 3, "If forgetting is the cause, a simple reminder may fix it more cheaply and with fewer risks. As practical judgement, test a non-AI option against the current baseline first.", "scFR1"),
    S("fr037", "frame", "The team does build a model that predicts likely misses. How should it decide the cost of each kind of error?", ["By weighing both errors equally as the default","With clinicians and patients, weighing a wasted slot against a double-booked attendee","By letting the sponsor choose the cheaper error","By following the vendor's recommended settings"], 1, "The two errors have different consequences, one for efficiency and one for patient care. People who understand both sides should weigh them in the success definition.", "scFR1"),
    S("fr038", "frame", "Should the system double-book slots automatically, or show staff the risk and let them decide?", ["Double-book automatically, since it saves staff a lot of time","Double-book automatically for all patients flagged high risk","Show staff the risk, since patient care is at stake","Double-book automatically only on the busiest days each week"], 2, "The stakes for patients are high and staff carry responsibility. Augmentation keeps a person deciding and can later expand if evidence supports it.", "scFR1"),
    S("fr039", "frame", "The booking export contains health-related details. What is the right next step?", ["Involve the privacy team before using that data for a prediction model","Use the data now and tell the privacy team at launch","Remove the patient names from the export and then continue with the model build","Ask the data team to decide if it is acceptable"], 0, "Health-related personal data is potentially regulated. The PO does not give legal or privacy rulings and should involve the privacy team and compliance specialists early.", "scFR1"),
    M("fr040", "frame", 2, "Which TWO should the team write into the scope of the first release?", ["Which appointment types are included","The vendor that supplied the data export","The names of staff who attended workshops","A promise to extend to all clinics later","Which clinics have evidence behind the results"], [0,4], "A scope statement follows what the system can do and the context where it was shown to work. Appointment types and evidence-backed locations are scope. Vendors, attendees and promises are not.", "scFR1"),
    S("fr041", "frame", "About half of the questions are the same dozen policy questions with fixed answers. Which approach should be tried first for these?", ["A model that writes a fresh answer for every question asked","A model that learns from past tickets with no human review","A voice interface that reads the policy aloud to employees","A maintained FAQ or rules drawn from the current policy"], 3, "Fixed, repeated questions are where PAIR says a rule or simple solution is easier to build, explain and maintain. As practical judgement, try a maintained source first; it also stays correct when policy wording changes.", "scFR2"),
    S("fr042", "frame", "The sponsor's measure is 'fewer tickets'. Which success measure is best?", ["Number of questions the assistant receives","Correct answers and users who need no repeat contact, agreed with finance","Number of tickets closed per support agent","Minutes users spend chatting with the assistant"], 1, "Fewer tickets can be achieved by unhelpful answers. A success measure should reflect correct, resolved answers and be agreed by the people involved.", "scFR2"),
    M("fr043", "frame", 2, "Which TWO are non-monetary costs of wrong answers from the assistant?", ["The licence fee for the assistant","Extra finance staff hours to correct claims","Employees lose trust in the help channel","The cost of the pilot's hosting","Employees feel stress when claims are rejected"], [2,4], "MAP 3.2 asks for non-monetary costs of errors to be examined. Trust and stress are real costs without a price tag. Fees, hours and hosting are monetary.", "scFR2"),
    S("fr044", "frame", "The other half of the questions are unusual cases that need a judgement by the finance team. What is the best design?", ["Let the assistant decide and finance audit a sample","Let the assistant decide and employees appeal","Keep finance specialists as decision makers and have the assistant prepare the facts for them","Stop accepting unusual cases to cut the workload"], 2, "Judgement cases are high in cost of error and hard to put into rules, so augmentation fits: the assistant gathers facts and a specialist decides.", "scFR2"),
    S("fr045", "frame", "What should the written scope say about the assistant's limits?", ["It answers from the current policy only and routes other questions to finance","It answers any expense question as best it can","It answers policy questions and also offers its own advice on how exceptions should be handled","It answers from any source the model finds helpful"], 0, "A scope based on what the system can do reliably, with a route to people for the rest, protects users and keeps the system within its tested conditions.", "scFR2"),
    S("fr046", "frame", "A manager asks the assistant to also advise on the tax treatment of expenses. What is the best response?", ["Add it, since the assistant already handles expenses","Add it with a note that advice is general","Add it and review complaints monthly","Involve finance, tax and legal specialists and keep tax advice out of scope until they agree"], 3, "Tax treatment is specialist territory and the PO cannot settle it alone. A new use goes through its own framing with the right experts, and stays out of scope until then.", "scFR2")
  );

  Object.assign(window.AIPO.why, {
    "fr001": ["This starts from the technology and optimises for a demo, not for a need.","A backlog of model features assumes AI is the answer before any problem is confirmed.","Understanding the problem and today's workaround is the foundation for any later choice, AI or not.","Choosing a supplier commits to a solution before the problem is understood."],
    "fr002": ["It starts from the problem and asks for AI's unique contribution, which is the PAIR framing.","This is the technology-first form that PAIR advises moving away from.","Popularity of an approach says nothing about whether users need it.","Copying a rival begins with their solution, not with your users' problem."],
    "fr003": ["A solution is chosen before the problem is known, and AI is only one guess.","This acts on one view, which may be wrong or may miss the real cause.","Recording an unevidenced claim as confirmed makes an opinion look like a fact.","Evidence turns an opinion into a defined problem and may change which solution is right."],
    "fr004": ["Mapping is neutral. It may well show that a simpler fix is enough.","The map exposes real pain points and candidate steps, whatever solution follows.","A process map is not a dataset, and data needs are a separate question.","Mapping works best alongside conversations and observation, not instead of them."],
    "fr005": ["Observing real use shows needs and workarounds that people may not describe.","Competitor features show solutions, not your users' problems.","Engineer preferences are about techniques, not user needs.","Data on current behaviour complements what people say and do.","Requirements for a model before user contact assume the solution."],
    "fr006": ["Building first spends the most effort before learning whether people accept the idea.","Stakeholder guesses are not the reactions of the people who will use it.","A human-run simulation is cheap and shows real reactions before any build cost (general practice).","Hoping users adapt skips learning and risks building something unwanted."],
    "fr007": ["A rule does the job and is simpler to explain and maintain, which is the better product choice.","A fixed format has few edge cases a rule cannot handle, so the gain is small.","Team learning is a poor reason for the user to take on extra cost and complexity.","A comparison skips the prior question of whether AI is needed at all."],
    "fr008": ["Static information is the textbook case where a simple solution wins.","Predictability is the value here, which AI can weaken.","A fixed table is deterministic, so a rule is simpler and easier to explain.","Evolving, rare patterns are hard to capture in fixed rules, so learning from data adds unique value."],
    "fr009": ["A higher average hides the severe cost of the rare bad outcome.","A small gain does not justify severe error costs, so the simpler approach is better.","Learning from harm after launch accepts the cost on users who did not choose it.","A slight rise in success does not remove the severity of the remaining errors."],
    "fr010": ["Personalisation is a typical case where AI adds unique value.","AI cannot always give a full account of every step, so a rule suits this need.","Understanding varied language is hard to cover with rules.","Recognising a whole class cannot be programmed item by item.","Predictable behaviour is easier to guarantee with fixed logic."],
    "fr011": ["A rival's launch is a market signal, not a risk-reducing alternative.","Effort already spent is not a reason to keep an approach.","The subcategory names non-AI alternatives as part of the response to risk.","Leaderboard rank is unrelated to whether AI is the right approach."],
    "fr012": ["A simple version delivers value on time and keeps the AI option open.","Delay gives up the value of the time-critical need.","A rushed AI release tests the riskiest option on real users.","Stopping removes value users could already have."],
    "fr013": ["A vision statement repeats the claim without testing it.","Other products' features do not show value for your users.","Training a model first commits effort before the need is shown.","A baseline comparison tests whether AI is really needed."],
    "fr014": ["Staff find no enjoyment or social value in this task, so augmentation adds little.","It matches the PAIR signs for automation and keeps a route for oversight.","A boring task with agreed rules is the classic candidate for automation.","Even automated tasks should allow people to step in."],
    "fr015": ["Complaints arrive after harm to the relationship and trust.","Partial automation still removes the counsellor's role in some cases.","It respects the personal responsibility and meaning of the task while still giving help.","Asking for adaptation after the fact reverses the sensible order."],
    "fr016": ["Oversight options let people catch and correct errors in real time.","No AI can promise zero errors, and such a claim sets false expectations.","A control only engineers can use gives affected users no say.","A report to the sponsor is too slow to prevent harm to users."],
    "fr017": ["Tedious work is a typical automation candidate.","Risky work is better handed to a machine where possible.","Taking the task away would remove what people value about it.","People often want to stay in control when the stakes are high for them.","A machine can fill a skill gap people cannot close."],
    "fr018": ["Adoption problems are predictable if the people affected were not heard.","Hiding the system undermines trust and transparency.","Dropping the goal outright skips the chance to meet it another way.","It takes the signal seriously and looks for a way to meet the goal without disrupting that task."],
    "fr019": ["Naming a person afterwards does not meet an obligation that applies to the process.","Legal questions go to the people qualified to answer them, before design is fixed.","Data science is not the right specialism for a legal requirement.","A sponsor's reading is not an expert view of the legal position."],
    "fr020": ["Pricing is a business decision, not the model's objective.","Reach is a business target, not a model objective.","It defines success and failure for the model, steering its behaviour.","The term is not about team incentives."],
    "fr021": ["Different disciplines spot different pitfalls, which is the PAIR recommendation.","Technical skill alone misses user and product consequences.","One person cannot see the user, technical and product effects.","Defaults are not tailored to your users, and the choice is not the vendor's alone."],
    "fr022": ["Equal weights are a tempting default that PAIR warns against.","Reducing false alarms alone may raise missed urgent messages.","The balance is a product decision, not a by-product of training.","Real consequences differ, so the weights should reflect them."],
    "fr023": ["Time spent can rise for bad reasons, such as distraction or friction.","It tests second-order effects, which is the PAIR check for a narrow measure.","Two activity counts share the same weakness.","Sign-ups are a different activity count and say nothing about benefit."],
    "fr024": ["A measurable threshold makes success and failure clear.","The model name is an implementation detail.","A named action turns the threshold into a decision.","A date is a schedule item, not a success statement.","Vendors considered are background, not a measure of success."],
    "fr025": ["Dismissing a group as an edge case leaves harm in place.","An average can stay healthy while one group is poorly served.","It listens to those affected and acts, as PAIR recommends.","Removing data hides the problem rather than fixing it."],
    "fr026": ["These are core items in the MAP 1.1 description.","These are build details, not context of use.","These are delivery facts, not the intended use and impacts.","These are commercial facts, not the context of use."],
    "fr027": ["Settings are chosen later and are not a framing decision.","Benefits must be examined and documented, not assumed.","Attendance is administrative.","Costs of errors need the same attention as benefits.","A tracking tool is a delivery detail."],
    "fr028": ["No complaints is not the same as demonstrated value.","Expanding a feature of unknown value scales the uncertainty.","More data does not tell you what value is being delivered.","It restates the value and context so the next decision is based on evidence."],
    "fr029": ["The PO does not own the risk decision alone.","As good practice, tolerance is agreed with those accountable for risk before building, not set by the PO alone.","Reviewing after release puts users at risk first.","Engineers can inform the rate but do not own the risk decision."],
    "fr030": ["Testing finds errors but does not weigh what they cost.","Without costs, nobody can show benefits outweigh them.","A balanced view of benefits and costs is needed to judge the trade-off.","Stronger wording does not fix a missing analysis."],
    "fr031": ["Scope should follow what the system can do and the context it is used in.","Ambition is not evidence of capability.","Budget affects planning but is not the basis the subcategory names.","A competitor's scope does not fit your system or context."],
    "fr032": ["The same model does not guarantee the same quality in a new language.","A notice shifts the risk to users without evidence.","Making users the testers is not a plan for people who rely on it.","As practical judgement, scope should reflect what has been shown to work; the language can be added after evaluation."],
    "fr033": ["Testing within scope is still needed, and edge behaviour still occurs.","Defined limits help people know where to go for other needs.","Users still need a route when the system cannot help.","The aim is honesty about limits, not perceived capability."],
    "fr034": ["Being live in one context does not make it suited to another.","A disclaimer does not address the harm of wrong answers.","A new use deserves its own framing and expert input before it is in scope.","Complaints are late evidence for a high-stakes topic."],
    "fr035": ["Evidence about causes shapes which solution, AI or not, is worth trying.","Training starts from a solution and from data that may not hold the cause.","Demos show products, not the cause of the problem.","Requirements for double-booking lock in a solution without evidence."],
    "fr036": ["A prediction model does not remove forgetting and adds cost and risk.","A chatbot adds a new interaction, not a reminder.","A new booking page does not address the cause.","A simple non-AI change addresses the stated cause and is easy to measure."],
    "fr037": ["Equal weighting is a default that rarely fits real consequences.","The people who live with each error help set the weights.","Cost to the clinic is only part of the picture, and one voice is not enough.","Vendor settings are not tailored to this clinic's patients."],
    "fr038": ["Time saved does not outweigh patient harm from wrong double-bookings.","A high-risk flag is still a prediction that can be wrong.","Augmenting keeps accountable humans in the decision where patient impact is high.","Busy days raise the cost of a wrong double-booking."],
    "fr039": ["The privacy team is the right specialist, and early involvement avoids rework and harm.","Telling them at launch is too late to change the design.","Removing names alone may not address the privacy question.","A data team is not the privacy authority."],
    "fr040": ["Naming included appointment types sets clear limits.","A data source is not a scope boundary.","Attendance is administrative.","A promise to expand is a plan, not scope based on evidence.","Tying scope to clinics with evidence reflects what is known to work."],
    "fr041": ["Fresh generation adds variation where fixed answers are wanted.","Learning from tickets may repeat outdated answers when policy changes.","A voice layer changes the interface, not the quality of answers.","A simple maintained source suits fixed answers and is easy to update."],
    "fr042": ["Volume of questions shows use, not help.","It tests whether users got right answers, and it is defined across functions.","Closure counts reflect support activity, not user outcomes.","Long chats may signal confusion, not value."],
    "fr043": ["A fee is a monetary cost.","Staff hours can be priced.","Loss of trust is a lasting non-monetary cost.","Hosting is a monetary cost.","Stress and friction for people are non-monetary costs."],
    "fr044": ["Sampling finds errors after the harm has occurred.","Appeals put the burden on employees after a wrong decision.","It keeps accountable specialists deciding and still saves their time.","Refusing cases removes value users need."],
    "fr045": ["Limited scope with a handover is honest and safe.","Answering anything stretches beyond tested conditions.","Advice on exceptions needs finance judgement, not the assistant.","Uncontrolled sources risk wrong or outdated answers."],
    "fr046": ["Being related to expenses does not make it safe to extend.","A general note does not address wrong answers.","Monthly complaint review is late and slow for harmful errors.","The right specialists must frame the use and its limits before it is in scope."]
  });

  window.AIPO.cards.push(
    { id: "frc01", domain: "frame", front: "What question should replace 'Can we use AI to ...?'", back: "'How might we solve this problem?', followed by 'Can AI solve it in a unique way?'\nTrap: starting from the technology, so every option except AI is ignored." },
    { id: "frc02", domain: "frame", front: "Name three ways to find the real user problem.", back: "Talk to people, observe their behaviour and look through data.\nTrap: relying on one stakeholder's opinion." },
    { id: "frc03", domain: "frame", front: "What is a Wizard of Oz test?", back: "A test where a person quietly performs the automated step so you see how users react before building. PAIR names the test; this description is the common usage.\nTrap: building the full model first and asking about acceptance afterwards." },
    { id: "frc04", domain: "frame", front: "What do MAP 1.1 and MAP 1.4 ask the team to establish?", back: "Intended purposes, users, expectations and impacts (1.1), and a clearly defined or re-evaluated business value (1.4).\nTrap: assuming the value of an old feature is still known." },
    { id: "frc05", domain: "frame", front: "Why is a rule or heuristic often a good answer?", back: "It is usually easier to build, explain, debug and maintain, and it may work as well as AI.\nTrap: treating 'no AI' as failure." },
    { id: "frc06", domain: "frame", front: "Give two situations where AI is probably the better fit.", back: "Personalised recommendations, predicting events, natural language, recognising a whole class, or rare changing events such as fraud.\nTrap: applying the list to tasks where predictability matters." },
    { id: "frc07", domain: "frame", front: "Give two situations where AI is probably not better.", back: "Predictability is the value, information is static, costly errors outweigh a small gain, or full transparency is required.\nTrap: accepting a small average gain despite severe rare errors." },
    { id: "frc08", domain: "frame", front: "What does MANAGE 2.1 ask about alternatives?", back: "That viable non-AI alternatives are considered along with resources when managing risk.\nTrap: comparing AI options only with each other." },
    { id: "frc09", domain: "frame", front: "When is automation usually preferred?", back: "When the task is boring, repetitive, awkward or dangerous, people lack the skill, or people agree on the correct method.\nTrap: automating without any way for people to oversee or undo." },
    { id: "frc10", domain: "frame", front: "When is augmentation usually preferred?", back: "When people enjoy the task, carry personal responsibility, face high stakes or find preferences hard to describe.\nTrap: automating a task that people value doing." },
    { id: "frc11", domain: "frame", front: "What should almost always be offered even for automated tasks?", back: "A way for people to oversee and step in, such as preview, edit or undo.\nTrap: giving control only to engineers." },
    { id: "frc12", domain: "frame", front: "Staff say they do not want a task helped by AI. What next?", back: "Treat it as a signal not to disrupt that task: explore why and look for other ways to meet the goal.\nTrap: proceeding and hoping they adapt." },
    { id: "frc13", domain: "frame", front: "What is the reward function in PAIR terms?", back: "How an AI model tells right from wrong outputs, which shapes what it optimises.\nTrap: confusing it with team incentives or business targets." },
    { id: "frc14", domain: "frame", front: "What is the difference between a false positive and a false negative?", back: "A false positive is a wrong 'yes' such as a false alarm. A false negative is a wrong 'no' such as a missed case.\nTrap: weighing both equally by default." },
    { id: "frc15", domain: "frame", front: "Why test the precision and recall balance with users?", back: "The two trade off and there is no way around it, so users help find the right point.\nTrap: demanding the maximum of both." },
    { id: "frc16", domain: "frame", front: "What form does PAIR suggest for a success and failure statement?", back: "If a specific metric crosses a meaningful threshold, we will take a specific action.\nTrap: a metric with no threshold or no action." },
    { id: "frc17", domain: "frame", front: "What do MAP 3.1 and MAP 3.2 ask to be documented?", back: "Potential benefits (3.1) and potential costs of errors, including non-monetary costs, linked to risk tolerance (3.2).\nTrap: a business case with benefits only." },
    { id: "frc18", domain: "frame", front: "Give examples of non-monetary costs of AI errors.", back: "Lost trust, distress, unfair treatment or harm to a person.\nTrap: counting only fees, hosting and staff time." },
    { id: "frc19", domain: "frame", front: "Who decides risk tolerance?", back: "MAP 1.5 asks that tolerances are determined and documented; GOVERN 2.3 says executive leadership takes responsibility for AI risk decisions. As judgement, the PO supplies evidence and escalates.\nTrap: the PO setting it alone." },
    { id: "frc20", domain: "frame", front: "What does MAP 3.3 ask about scope?", back: "That targeted application scope is specified and documented from capability, context and categorisation.\nTrap: letting scope drift to untested users or tasks." }
  );
})();
